    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Alex Wicker is used to getting puzzled looks from "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "baristas",
            "zh": "咖啡店店员"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " when he visits his local café. His order is “unusual”—black coffee."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "“I just ask for ‘coffee’ without any extras,” says Wicker. “But this has become impossible without going through a round of 20 questions with the barista.”"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "These days, products like "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "iced lattes",
            "zh": "拿铁咖啡"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " and "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "pistachio",
            "zh": "开心果"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " cream "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "cold brews",
            "zh": "冷萃咖啡"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " have become all too popular. Black-coffee drinkers like Wicker are becoming rare. They often find themselves waiting patiently for their simple drink. After all, the complex drinks ordered by those ahead of them take time to prepare."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "At Starbucks, the world’s largest coffee "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "chain",
            "ref": "v-chain"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", 80 per cent of customers add milk, sugar, or other extras to their coffee. Every year, the company sells about $1 billion in coffee drinks with add-ons. The number has doubled since 2019."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Howard Schultz, Starbucks’ longtime leader, "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "initially",
            "ref": "v-initially"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "opposed",
            "ref": "v-oppose"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " offering sweetened drinks. He still prefers his coffee black. Some black-coffee drinkers take pride in enjoying its strong, bitter taste without "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "masking",
            "ref": "v-mask"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " it with milk or sugar."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p6",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Some Starbucks baristas have to run back and forth across the preparation areas to make complex drinks. They wish more customers could appreciate the chain’s coffee in its purest form. But many people don’t seem to know what black coffee is anymore. Some customers order black coffee, baristas say, and later complain that it doesn’t come with sugar or cream."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "i1",
        "type": "imageRef",
        "image": "img1"
      },
      {
        "id": "p7",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Abbey Clifford is also a black coffee drinker. She says she keeps getting her black coffee with cream. “Maybe they think I mean plain coffee, and that plain means just a little cream,” she said. “But that’s not what black coffee means at all.”"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p8",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Some Starbucks workers say they sometimes wonder whether black coffee should include sugar or room for milk. When people order black coffee, they ask whether they want it sweetened, just to make sure."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p9",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "“It’s the biggest debate,” said Brooke Cross, a Starbucks barista. “What is black coffee?”"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
